    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, the acceptance test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` passes).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required rejecting an E.164 telephone with **400** when its national-number length is wrong for the country code (`+61` \u2192 9 national digits, `+1` \u2192 10).\n\n**Key findings from investigation:**\n- Telephone normalization already runs in the mapper (`E164Telephone.toE164`), producing the stored E.164 form.\n- Relying on `Owner`'s persist-time `@Pattern` doesn't work: it fires at JPA flush and the catch-all `@ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)` turns it into **500**, not 400. I verified this empirically.\n- The codebase's established idiom is a small `Owner*Policy` class with a `@ResponseStatus` exception plus a matching `@ExceptionHandler` in the advice (needed because the catch-all otherwise intercepts it \u2014 also verified empirically).\n\n**Changes (3 files, all small/cohesive units to keep structural impact low):**\n1. **`OwnerTelephoneLengthPolicy.java`** (new, tiny class) \u2014 `requireValidNationalLength(digits)` enforcing `+61`\u219211 total / `+1`\u219211 total, throwing a nested `@ResponseStatus(BAD_REQUEST)` `InvalidNationalNumberLengthException`. Other country codes fall back to the existing generic rule.\n2. **`E164Telephone.java`** \u2014 one added call to the policy after the existing digit-range check; no growth of its logic, no new branches in the class it lives in.\n3. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`** \u2014 one new small handler mapping the exception to 400, alongside the existing peer handlers.\n\nI deliberately avoided touching the large `Owner` model and `OwnerRestControllerV1` classes, keeping every changed function simple and the surrounding complexity per change minimal.",
